Every package has two independent classifications: where it comes from and how the compiler obtains its implementation.

### Package origin

Origin describes ownership and distribution:

- **Core**: compiler-owned packages such as `@core/io` and `@core/math`.
- **Builder**: packages selected by a project builder, including `@html` and `@web/canvas`.
- **ProjectLocal**: scoped `+*.moth` support packages, the project-root package facade and annotated project-local JavaScript files.
- **Dependency**: reserved for future package-system support.

### Package backing

Backing describes implementation:

- **MothSource**: one or more Moth modules resolved through the source package registry.
- **ExternalBinding**: typed functions, constants and opaque types implemented outside Moth.

Backing is semantic, not a file-format choice. Precompiling or emitting a `MothSource` package as Wasm keeps it `MothSource`. Imported WIT components are `ExternalBinding` packages.

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Package**]
        [: **Origin**]
        [: **Backing**]
    ]

    [td: [:`@html`] [: Builder] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [:`@core/collections`, `@core/io`, `@core/math`, `@core/text`, `@core/random`, `@core/time`] [: Core] [: ExternalBinding]]
    [td: [:`@web/canvas`, `@web/graphics`] [: Builder] [: ExternalBinding]]
    [td: [: Scoped `+*.moth` support package] [: ProjectLocal] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [: Project-root `+*.moth` facade] [: ProjectLocal] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [: Annotated project-local `.js` file] [: ProjectLocal] [: ExternalBinding]]
]

Origin and backing don't change dependency clause syntax or module visibility. Source-backed packages contain ordinary modules with strict `export:` public surfaces. Binding-backed packages expose typed external symbols registered by the builder.

### Prelude is visibility policy

The prelude is implicit dependency policy, not an origin or backing. It exposes the bare `io` namespace as an alias to `@core/io`. Package metadata belongs to `@core/io`, while bare-name visibility belongs to the prelude.

### Builder-selected availability

Builders provide implementations and additional packages without redefining
Core semantics. Official browser and native environments provide the canonical
Core catalogue, including its documented host-capability gates. Custom builders
may provide a subset or no public Core implementation. Reserved Core identities
and the `io` prelude keep their meaning even when an operation is unavailable.
The progress matrix records current implementation gaps separately from this
design contract.

The future official package manager classifies managed Moth-source packages as
no-core or Core-dependent over their complete dependency closure. Official
certified environments may consume both. Custom builders receive no-core
packages only, even when they use Lappet or implement all Core APIs. Certification
is a Moth project decision, not inferred from matching signatures.

Compiler-internal helpers and the unused `io` prelude do not make a package
Core-dependent. No-core still requires support for the language features it
executes. Managed Builder-dependent and foreign-code packages are deferred.
Local and builder-supplied packages retain their normal compiler rules. See
@../lappet (Lappet) for scope and the package-manager design.

### Binding-backed package constraints

Binding-backed packages expose free functions, constants and opaque types. They don't expose source-style receiver methods. Use a source-backed wrapper type for method-style APIs. External calls are positional-only because named arguments and default parameters are source-function features.

The HTML-Wasm mode has a stricter backend surface. A reachable JS-backed package needs a Wasm lowering before that backend can use it.

### No package manager yet

There is no remote fetching, lockfile or version resolution yet. Use `+*.moth` support packages for project-local source-backed packages, builder-provided package dependency clauses for built-in capabilities and annotated `.js` wrappers for direct browser integration.

### Concepts related to package origins and backing

- For project-local packages see @#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).
- For dependency path families see @#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
- For Core package API details see @./core (Core packages).
